/* =====================================================================
   LAYER 9 — THE SULPHUR DEEP
   The bottom of everything. A sulphur castle raised by nine hydrothermal
   vents, bright canary-yellow Frasch/Louisiana-sulphur crust, the throne-ward
   of hell — and it is FULL OF PIG DEMONS, the kidnapper's kind, ruled by their
   lord. Register: infernal grandeur + the chemical/poison band come full circle
   (the brimstone is both spiritual and literal; the top of the descent was
   poison, and here at the bottom poison and damnation are the same thing).
   Holds SIGIL-FRAGMENT 3 — collecting it completes the Unbinding Sigil, and the
   Hellmouth then offers the unbound exit (EndingUnbound).

   Hub = TheVentField. Enter from L8 (TheRunoff → TheBrimstoneShore); ascend via
   TheRunback. No layer below — this is the floor of hell. Depths = 9.
   ===================================================================== */


/* ---------- ENTRY ---------- */

:: TheBrimstoneShore [hell]
<<corrupt "head" "pig" 3>>
The runoff carries you down the last drop and spits you onto a shore at the bottom of
everything — and the bottom of everything is *yellow*. Not the red of the furnaces, not
the organ-pink of the belly, but a vast bright canary yellow, the yellow of a warning, of
a quarantine flag, of sulphur: a crust of it underfoot, sublimed and re-deposited and built
up over an age into dunes and crags and the far, impossible bulk of a castle, all of it the
same uniform poison-bright yellow, lit from within and below by the nine vents whose heat
made it. The air is thick enough to lean on and every breath is a small chemical injury.

This is the throne-ward of hell, the deepest place there is, and the smell of it — under the
brimstone, the old familiar smell — is wet pork. Their smell. This is where the pig-things
come from, and where their lord keeps the bottom of the world.

The runoff that brought you down here goes on past your feet and soaks away into the yellow
without a sound, the way everything down here arrives and stays. There is no current onward,
no slope to follow, only the crust crunching underfoot like burnt sugar and that far castle
sitting in the haze with the patience of a thing that knows you have nowhere else the descent
could possibly take you. You have, you understand, finally hit bottom. The only way that is
not down from here is the way you came, and that way is closed behind you, fallen with the
water.

<<link "Onto the field of vents">><<goto "TheVentField">><</link>>


/* ---------- HUB ---------- */

:: TheVentField [hell]
<<nobr>>
Nine vents roar in a rough ring across the yellow plain — nine throats into the actual
fundament, each jetting a column of superheated sulphur-stink and shimmering heat-haze up
into the dark, and it is their nine breaths, blown across an age, that raised the castle:
the sulphur sublimes off the vents and re-freezes wherever it cools, so the whole canary
fortress is not built but *grown*, vent-breath turned to yellow stone, a Frasch-bright crag
of brimstone the size of a cathedral with the lord of the pigs somewhere at the heart of it.

Out from the ring the plain spreads in every direction, and as your eyes settle to the
heat-shimmer you start to read it — the safe shelves where the crust has gone cold and pale,
the bright thin reaches where it has not, and past them only fume and colour and the
far dark the haze swallows, every quarter of it a different shade of the same
poison. The roar of the nine fills it all, a sound so total it becomes a kind of silence, the
held breath of the bottom of the world going in and out, in and out.

Wherever you turn, the castle is the thing your eye comes back to. It does not loom so much as
*wait*, squatting in the haze at the heart of the ring with its gate of standing fire, and the
longer you stand here the more the whole yellow plain feels like a courtyard laid out before
it — every road, every shelf, every cold seam in the crust, eventually bending in toward that
one door and the thing that keeps the bottom behind it.
<</nobr>>

<<if setup.hasSigil()>>
The two fragments you carry pull, hard, toward the castle — toward the third — and toward
each other, eager to be whole, hateful to all this yellow grandeur.
<</if>>

<<link "Toward the nine vents, at the ring's heart">><<goto "TheNineVents">><</link>>
<<link "Toward the castle gate, a curtain of sulphur-fire">><<goto "TheCastleGate">><</link>>
<<link "Across a field of fuming sulphur">><<goto "TheSolfatara">><</link>>
<<link "A stable shelf of cold yellow crust">><<goto "TheCrust">><</link>>
<<link "A fume-cut between two vents, a tribute-niche glittering past it">><<goto "TheFumeCut">><</link>>
<<link "Out on the crust, a lone tree, burning">><<goto "TheBurningTree">><</link>>
<<link "Back up the runback, toward the rendering">><<goto "TheRunback">><</link>>


/* ---------- ASCENT ---------- */

:: TheRunback [hell]
The runback is the only way up from the bottom — a stepped climb beside the falling runoff,
up out of the yellow into the furnace-red of the Works above, the chemical sear of the
brimstone giving way by degrees to the merely-hot stink of rendering, which after the Deep
counts as a cleaner air.

The steps are not cut but worn, scooped hollows in the crust where the runoff has chewed its
channel and where, you realize, other feet went up ahead of yours — not many, and not lately,
but some, scuffing the same shallow holds, leaning the same way into the same long grade. The
water falls beside you the whole climb, throwing a fine sulphur mist that beads cold on the
back of your neck and tastes, faintly, of the place you are leaving.

Climbing it you understand that you are doing the thing nothing down here does: leaving the
bottom under your own power, against the grain of a place where everything, always, only ever
goes down. Each step puts a little more red into the dark above and a little more distance
under the yellow behind, and the muscles of it ache in a way that is almost good, because ache
means effort, and effort means you have not yet lain down and let the crust decide.

<<link "Up, into the rendering works">><<goto "TheKillingFloor">><</link>>
<<link "Back down to the field of vents">><<goto "TheVentField">><</link>>


/* ---------- THE BURNING TREE (fire source: the branch that gates the L2 Fatberg) ---------- */

:: TheBurningTree [hell]
Out past the vents, alone on a shelf of bare yellow crust, there is a tree.

It should not be here — nothing grows in the Deep, nothing green has ever grown anywhere in
hell — and it is not green: it is black, and bare, and burning, every branch and twig of it
sheathed in a steady sulphur-blue flame that does not flicker and does not spread and does not,
you understand as you stand and watch, *consume*. The tree does not blacken further. The fire
does not gutter. It has been burning, exactly this way, since before the castle, and it will be
burning when the castle is dust — a fire that lasts, which is the one thing the bottom of hell
has that the long climb back up could ever want.

<<if setup.has("brand")>>
You have already taken your branch. It burns in your fist, steady and weightless and cold to
hold, and lights your way without warming it.
<<else>>
<<linkreplace "Break off a branch">>
<<acquire "brand">>
You set your hand to the lowest limb and it comes away easy, with a sound like a held breath
let go, and the break does not gutter — the branch burns on in your fist exactly as it burned
on the tree, steady sulphur-blue, weightless, cold to hold and hot enough, you suspect, to melt
through anything that ever clogged a drain. The tree seals the wound with a creak and burns on,
undiminished, the way it has, you somehow know, for every soul that ever needed a fire to climb by.
<</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

<<link "Back to the field of vents">><<goto "TheVentField">><</link>>


/* ---------- GATE (guards the throne / fragment 3) ---------- */

:: TheCastleGate [hell]
The way into the castle is a gate of standing fire — a curtain of burning sulphur drawn
across the only breach in the yellow walls, blue-hearted and roaring, throwing a heat that
crisps the hairs of your arm from a body's length back. To walk through it as you are is to
burn. But the crust is treacherous everywhere here, thin in places over the vents' heat, and
somewhere in the field there is a way the crust runs thick and safe right up under the fire,
a cold bridge through the hot gate, if you know where the vents have laid it.

<<if $crustBridge>>
You found it at the vents — the cold seam, the thick crust-tongue that runs in under the
flame where a vent's draught holds the fire up off the ground. You go in low and fast along
it, through the roaring blue, into the castle, and come up the far side singed and whole.

<<link "Into the castle, toward the throne">><<goto "TheThroneWard">><</link>>
<<else>>
You can't cross the fire safely without the crust-seam, and you haven't found it. Or you can
simply walk through the standing flame and let it take its price of you — burn through and
arrive in the castle cooked and lessened but *there*.

<<set _tlbl to "Walk through the sulphur-fire (" + setup.tollTag("torso", "pig", 26) + ")">><<link _tlbl>>
  <<corrupt "torso" "pig" 26>>
  <<goto "TheThroneWard">>
<</link>>
<<link "Go find the crust-bridge among the vents">><<goto "TheNineVents">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<link "A great yellow hall, along the wall">><<goto "TheYellowHall">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the field of vents">><<goto "TheVentField">><</link>>


/* ---------- THE THRONE / FRAGMENT 3 / the lord's offer ---------- */

:: TheThroneWard [hell]
The throne-ward is the heart of the yellow castle, and on a throne grown of vent-breath and
crowned in canary crystal sits the lord of the pigs — vast past the scale of the boar that
took you, vast as the act of taking itself, a sow-and-boar-and-neither thing of such
enormous and patient appetite that the whole farm of hell, the belly and the works and the
surface harvest, is simply its larder. It does not rise. It has no need. It only turns on you
a regard as old and incurious as weather, and into the bone of its own armrest, half-grown
over, is set the third fragment — the last third of the Sigil, kept here at the bottom like a
splinter the whole body of hell has tried for an age to wall off.

//Little harvest,// the lord says, in a voice you feel in the sulphur-crust under your feet.
//You came all the way down. So few do. Take the cold mark if you like — it was never ours,
it only itches — or stay, and be more than harvest. I am old. I could use an heir who knows
the way to the surface, where the farm grows thickest.//

<<if $sigil.includes("sigil_deep")>>
The socket in the armrest is empty, the proud yellow crystal already creeping to close it.
You have all three. The lord watches you hold them, and does not stop you, and that — you
understand, backing away — is the most frightening thing it could possibly do.
<<else>>
<<link "Cut the third fragment from the lord's own throne">>
  <<set $sigil.push("sigil_deep")>>
  <<corrupt "armR" "pig" 5>>
  <<goto "TheThroneWard">>
<</link>>
<<link "Take the other offer. Stay. Be the lord's heir.">><<goto "EndingHeir">><</link>>
<</if>>

<<if $sigil.includes("sigil_deep")>>
<<if setup.hasSigil()>>
Three. The Sigil is whole, and pulls toward the long way up and out — toward the front door
of hell, the mouth, where a whole mark could make the teeth stand aside.

<</if>>
<</if>>
<<link "Back out through the castle gate">><<goto "TheCastleGate">><</link>>


/* ---------- REST ---------- */

:: TheCrust [hell]
A shelf of old crust, far enough from the vents that it has gone cold — a pale yellow
table of sublimed sulphur, set hard as sandstone, cool to the touch, still. The vents'
roar is a distance off here, and the air is merely bad rather than actively wounding, and
the crust holds your weight without the sickening give it has everywhere nearer the heat.

Close to, the shelf is not bare. The cold has let things gather that the heat
elsewhere drives off: a drift of yellow dust in the lee of a crust-ridge, the old scuffs of
others who found this same island and rested on it, a smoothness worn into the stone where
bodies have sat exactly here, doing exactly this. You are not the first to wash up on the one
cold rock and shake. The proof of it is pressed into the floor under you.

It is the closest thing to safe ground at the bottom of the world: a cold yellow island in
the brimstone, where for a little while nothing is trying to render or breed or burn or eat
you, and you can sit, and shake, and remember that you came down here on purpose, for a
reason, and that the reason was *up*.

<<if $hp lt $maxhp>><<run setup.passTime(setup.REST_TIME)>><</if>><<set $hp to $maxhp>>

<<link "Across a walk of yellow gypsum">><<goto "TheGypsumWalk">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the field of vents">><<goto "TheVentField">><</link>>


/* ---------- CACHES ---------- */

:: TheSulphurHoard [hell]
The castle keeps a hoard, the way the deep things all keep hoards, and the lord's is
sulphur made jewel: native crystals grown clear and golden in the vents' slow heat, the one
beautiful thing the poison makes, racked and heaped and glittering.

Closer, the scale of it opens out — not a chest of treasure but a chamber of it, crystals the
size of teeth and crystals the size of doors, faceted and lamp-yellow and lit from inside by
nothing, throwing back the vent-glow in ten thousand small fires. They have grown into and over
each other the way the whole castle grew, slow and patient and never once disturbed, and the
floor crunches with the shards of the ones that grew too heavy and fell. Nothing here was placed.
It accreted, the way wealth accretes, by simply never spending.

And salted through it, because down here everything is salted through with it, the cold coin of
the farm's long trade — dull metal among the golden glitter, the only thing in the room a hand
from the world above would know how to use, and the only thing in the room nobody down here ever
wants.

<<if $sulphurHoardLooted>>
You've been back for the coin and left the crystal. It is beautiful, and it is sulphur, and you
have learned to leave the beautiful things of hell exactly where they lie.
<<else>>
The cold coin of the farm's long trade is salted through the hoard, bright among the canary-
yellow jewels of crusted sulphur — nobody guarding what nobody down here can spend, for whoever's
moved to come back and take it.
<</if>>

<<link "Toward the court of the pigs">><<goto "TheCourt">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the yellow hall">><<goto "TheYellowHall">><</link>>


:: TheVentMouth [hell]
At the lip of one of the nine vents the heat comes up at you like a wall, a column of yellow
breath roaring out of a throat you cannot see the bottom of, and you stand at the edge of it
the way you would stand at the edge of a cliff — leaning back, eyes streaming, the air itself
shoving.

The lip is rimmed in a slow lacework of crust the vent has built of its own exhalations, fluted
and frilled and growing as you watch, and packed into that rind, half-cemented, is the trade's
leavings — coin and trinket and the small metal sins of the damned, blown clear of the throat
by the vent's breath and re-settled in a crust-rimmed apron around its mouth. The metal has gone
the colours that metal goes in fire, blued and strawed and blackened, the value cooked out of it
and only the shape left.

It is too hot to have been gathered, which is why it is still here; and it is cooling now at the
edges, where the apron meets the colder crust, where a quick hand could chance it before the
vent's next breath drove the heat back out.

<<if $ventMouthLooted>>
You've taken what the vent gave up and your fingertips have the blisters to show for it. The
vent breathes on, indifferent, having barely noticed lending you its leavings.
<<else>>
Coin and trinket and the small metal sins of the damned lie cooling in the crust-rimmed apron
round the vent's mouth — too hot to have been gathered, cool enough now at the edges that a
quick hand could chance it, if a quick hand came back this way for it.
<</if>>

<<link "Back among the nine vents">><<goto "TheNineVents">><</link>>
<<link "Toward a single great fumarole">><<goto "TheFumarole">><</link>>


:: TheTribute [hell]
A tribute-heap before the way to the court — what the belly and the works send down to the
lord, the bottom's cut of everything harvested above, piled here at the threshold the way
offerings are piled at the foot of an altar nobody has seen but everybody feeds.

Walking up on it, you read the heap. Coin in drifts, yes — but coin is the least of it, the
small change at the edges. The mass of it is the chosen portions: the tributes of meat and
marrow, the cuts and the organs and the marbled best of the whole farm's killing, laid out
in rows that someone tends, that someone keeps in order, going slowly yellow at the rind where
the brimstone has begun to claim even this. It does not rot. Nothing down here is permitted
the dignity of rotting. It only crusts, and waits, and is added to.

This is what the whole machine of hell ultimately feeds — every drain and furnace and harvest
above ending here, in a heap at a doorway, the bottom's standing portion of everything that
ever went down. And nobody has counted it in a long age. The lord does not count. The lord
only, eventually, eats.

<<if $tributeLooted>>
You've taken your cut from the tribute-heap already. The rest you leave for the lord, which will
not miss what you took, which has, after all, the entire harvest of the world coming.
<<else>>
Coin lies in drifts among the offerings, and nobody's counted the tribute in a long age — a
little less of it would never be missed, by whoever's nerve is up for lifting from under the
lord's very nose when the thought of it walks them back here.
<</if>>

<<link "On, to the court of the pigs">><<goto "TheCourt">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the sulphur-hoard">><<goto "TheSulphurHoard">><</link>>


/* ---------- SNARE (thin crust) ---------- */

:: TheThinCrust [hell]
The yellow plain looks solid and is not. Out here, away from the cold shelf, the crust runs
thin over the vents' heat — a skin of sublimed sulphur over a drop into superheated nothing,
and it tells you nothing of where it's safe and where it's a held breath from giving way.

You feel it before you can name it: a softness underfoot that the thick crust never had, a
faint warmth coming up through the soles, here and there a flex underfoot, the give of a thin
lid over heat — the crust reminding you it is a skin, and not a floor. Somewhere under the bright even skin the vents are breathing,
and the only line between you and them is this — a hand's-width of frozen breath that held the
last weight to cross it and may or may not hold yours. The honest dread of it is correct and
survivable.

The danger is the other thing. Out in the middle of the thin yellow, where the heat comes up
through the crust like warmth through a blanket, there is a soft seductive temptation to simply
lie down on the warm bright skin of the world and rest, to stop holding your weight so carefully
off it, to let the warmth take you the way warmth takes you when you are this tired — and let the
crust decide.

<<link "Lie down on the warm bright crust.">><<goto "EndingSublimed">><</link>>
<<link "Pick your way back to thicker ground.">>
  <<soothe 5>>
  <<goto "TheCanaryCrust">>
<</link>>


/* ---------- COMBAT (the pig court) ---------- */

:: TheCourt [hell]
The court of the pigs is a wallowing hall of the lord's get — boars and sows of the first
rank, the overseers of the harvest, lounging in the brimstone heat the way courtiers lounge,
sprawled across crust-couches of yellow stone in a fug of pork and sulphur thick enough to
chew.

These are not the working pigs of the floors above. The change has run further in them, the
boar-shape gone fat and lordly and unhurried, tusks scrolled and yellowed with age, small eyes
set in folds of brimstone-glazed fat that follow you across the hall without the bodies
bothering to move. They have nowhere to be and nothing to do but oversee, and overseeing, just
now, means watching the thing that walked in. They take an interest in you that the working
pigs above never did, because down here you are not staff and not error but *trespass*, a
harvest-thing walking the throne-ward as if it had business there.

The interest sharpens. A stillness goes through the wallow, the lazy courtier-sprawl tightening
into something that has decided. One of the great boars heaves up to deal with the insult of you.

<<if $courtCleared>>
The boar you put down lies where the court drew back from it, and the rest watch you with a
wary, recalculating stillness — a harvest-thing that kills a courtier is a new sum they do
not have a column for. The way on, deeper into the castle, is open.

<<link "On, toward the yellow hall">><<goto "TheYellowHall">><</link>>
<<link "Back toward the tribute-heap">><<goto "TheTribute">><</link>>
<<else>>
<<scentof "pigdemon">>
<<link "Meet the court boar — settle it the way you settled the sty">>
  <<run setup.startFight("pigdemon", "TheCourtCleared")>>
  <<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<link "Withdraw from the court, while it merely watches">><<goto "TheTribute">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: TheCourtCleared [hell]
/* lands here after the court pig-demon fight; sets the flag, then folds onward */
<<set $courtCleared to true>>
The court-boar goes down with a squealing crash that the whole yellow castle hears, and for a
moment the brimstone hall is silent, the courtiers utterly still, recalculating. You have
killed the lord's own get in the lord's own house. It changes nothing about the long way out
and everything about how you will walk it.

<<link "On, toward the yellow hall">><<goto "TheYellowHall">><</link>>
<<link "Back toward the tribute-heap">><<goto "TheTribute">><</link>>


/* ---------- PASSAGE / ATMOSPHERE FILLER ---------- */

:: TheNineVents [hell]
Up close the nine vents are not nine holes but nine *mouths*, each roaring its column of
yellow heat, each rimmed in the slow lacework of crust its own breath builds, and walking
among them is walking through the lungs of the bottom of the world — deafening, blinding,
the heat-haze bending everything, the ground thrumming.

In the thick of them you lose your bearings entirely. The columns of breath bend the air so
that the castle swims and doubles and the far walls run like wet paint; the roar comes from
everywhere and nowhere; the crust under you trembles with the force of nine throats venting the
fundament, and there is a long bad moment where you cannot tell which way you came in or which
of the nine you are standing nearest. This is where the unwary blunder onto the thin places and
go down without a sound the roar would carry.

But the vents also lay the only safe roads in the Deep. Where two columns lean their draughts
together, the heat cancels in the gap between them and the crust there freezes thick and cold
and load-bearing — a pale seam running true through all that bending haze, a road a careful eye
can trace if it knows to look for cold instead of safety, for the place the fire leaves alone.

<<if not $crustBridge>>
<<linkreplace "Read the vents; trace the cold seam toward the castle gate">>
<<set $crustBridge to true>>
You watch the nine breaths long enough to learn their crossing, and there it is: a thick cold
tongue of crust running in under the very gate of fire, held safe by a vent's up-draught. You
have the bridge through the burning gate now, if you want it.
<</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
You have already traced the cold seam to the gate. The vents roar on, indifferent to having
shown you the one safe road across their own field.
<</if>>

<<link "Toward a single great fumarole">><<goto "TheFumarole">><</link>>
<<link "To the lip of a venting throat">><<goto "TheVentMouth">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the field's edge">><<goto "TheVentField">><</link>>


:: TheYellowHall [hell]
A hall of the castle, and the castle is all one substance: floor and wall and vault and the
very furniture grown of the same canary sulphur, sublimed and re-frozen into a brimstone
baroque of dripping crystal columns and crust-curtains and chandeliers of native gold-yellow
crystal that have never held a flame and never needed to, lit as everything down here is lit,
from within, from below, from the vents' eternal yellow burning.

Walking it, you see there is no seam anywhere — no join between floor and wall, no place where
one thing was set against another, because nothing here was set, nothing was built, it all simply
*grew*, the way frost grows on a pane overnight, flowing up out of the floor into the columns and
out along the vaults and down again in long frozen drips that never fell. Your footsteps wake no
echo the crust does not swallow. The chandeliers turn their slow facets in the up-glow and shed a
light that is not light so much as the absence of a darkness, even and shadowless and the colour
of warning.

It is beautiful the way a disease is beautiful under glass: intricate, patient, and made entirely
of the thing that will kill you.

<<link "Toward the throne-ward and its gate of fire">><<goto "TheCastleGate">><</link>>
<<link "A glittering hoard, off the hall">><<goto "TheSulphurHoard">><</link>>
<<link "Toward the court of the pigs">><<goto "TheCourt">><</link>>


:: TheSolfatara [hell]
A solfatara — a field where the ground itself fumes, ten thousand small mouths exhaling
sulphur, the crust between them painted in the impossible mineral colours the chemistry
throws: canary and ochre and a poisoned green and the white of pure sublimate, a garden of
brimstone laid out as far as the haze allows, beautiful and lethal and absolutely silent
under the distant roar of the great vents.

Picking through it, you learn to read the ground by colour. The canary is fresh sublimate, the
ochre is older and crustier, the poisoned green marks where a copper sweat has bled up through,
the chalk-white is the pure stuff laid down thickest — and each one fumes its own faint reek,
so that crossing the field is crossing through bands of differently-flavoured poison, the nose
sorting them out long after it should have given up and gone numb. Nothing grows here. Everything
is mineral. The only motion is the slow exhalation of the ten thousand mouths, and the only thing
that moves on purpose is you.

It is the chemical bottom the poisoned top of the whole descent was always draining toward, and
standing in it you feel the circle close: the foam on the storm-drains and the burning here are
the same substance, the same wrong, met at last at both ends.

<<if not $colourmanTalked>>
A man kneels in the colours, working them with his hands — grinding, sorting, the way a tradesman works
a material he knows in his bones — and looks up, unbothered, glad of someone to talk shop to in a place
where nothing grows and no one stays to chat.
<<linkreplace "Ask the man on his knees in the colours what he's at">><<nobr>><<set $colourmanTalked to true>>
//Orpiment, this one — king's-yellow, we called it; ground it for the gilders.// He rubs a thumb across
the crust, fond. //Lovely colour. Killed three of us I could name, and it never once //looked// like it
would. Realgar there, the red. The green's a copper thing. Forty year I ground the lot, bare-handed,
because the mask fogged and the foreman paid by the batch.// He shrugs. //You'll have stopped smelling
the brimstone by now. No? You will. That's the trade — first the colour gets in your nose, then it
//is// your nose, and the day you can't smell the poison is the day the poison can't tell you from
itself. I work better since. Hands don't shake, when there's nothing left in you to be afraid of.//
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "pig" 2>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
He's back at the colours and won't be drawn off them; he said his piece about the yellow, and there's a
whole floor of it wants minding.
<</if>>

<<link "Toward pools of standing vitriol">><<goto "TheVitriol">><</link>>
<<link "Across a stretch of canary crust">><<goto "TheCanaryCrust">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the field of vents">><<goto "TheVentField">><</link>>


:: TheFumarole [hell]
A single fumarole stands apart from the nine — a lesser vent, but old, its throat built up
over an age into a tall fluted chimney of yellow crust, exhaling a steady soft column you
could almost mistake for something gentle.

Up close it is the loveliest thing in the Deep, and the most modest: a slim yellow spire
twice your height, fluted like a cathedral pillar, its crust grown so fine and translucent
that the up-glow shines through it the colour of weak tea, and from its crown a column of
breath that does not roar but *sighs*, rising soft and straight into the dark. After the nine
great mouths it feels almost domestic, almost like a hearth, almost like something built for
company.

You warm yourself at the edge of its breath, because you are cold in your bones in a way the
heat does not reach, and the warmth is real and the breath is poison and you have, by now, made
your peace with wanting things that are both.

<<if not $hasWarmHide>>
Another soul is already here at the fumarole's edge, warming itself — but it has been here longer, and
the heat has grown //into// it: a glazed crust of yellow over its chest where the breath has played the
longest. It shifts to make room, companionable, and offers to show you how to take the warmth for keeps —
a hide that holds the vent's heat, so the bone-cold of the Deep never finds you again.
<<link "Let the breath grow its warmth into you, for keeps">>
<<set $hasWarmHide to true>><<corrupt "torso" "pig" 18>>
<<goto "TheVentMouth">>
<</link>>
<<link "Step back from the breath — keep your own cold">>
<<soothe 5>>
<<goto "TheNineVents">>
<</link>>
<<else>>
The Warmer has nothing left to teach you — the warmth is grown into you now, a glaze of it over your
chest, and the cold of the Deep keeps its distance the way it keeps its distance from the Warmer. It
nods you on, and goes back to its breath.

<<link "To the lip of a venting throat">><<goto "TheVentMouth">><</link>>
<<link "Back among the nine vents">><<goto "TheNineVents">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: TheVitriol [hell]
Pools of standing vitriol pock the low ground — sulphuric water collected clear and green and
faintly steaming in basins of its own etching, the runoff of the vents condensed to acid, so
pure and still and inviting-looking that the crust around each pool is white with the bones
of things that mistook it for water.

The bones tell the whole story if you read them, and you cannot help reading them: they ring
each pool in a tidemark, the small and the large, the four-legged and the two-, all of them
gone to clean white lime where the acid finished what the thirst began, all of them reaching
the same direction, toward the same green stillness, in the same last attitude of a thing
lowering its face to drink. The pools steam on, untroubled, every one of them as clear and
flat and lovely as the first cool water you ever wanted.

You are thirsty. You have been thirsty for what feels like a geological age. You look at the
clear green pools and the white bone rims and you do not drink, and the not-drinking is one
more small victory in a place that grants no large ones.

<<if not $vitriolTalked>>
A man crouches at the rim of the nearest pool, white to the elbows with the lime-bones of others who
drank — thirst-mad, and yet not drinking; he cannot stop watching the clear green water, and he has, it
turns out, learned the one thing worth learning down here by the simple method of having survived it.
<<linkreplace "Ask the rimmed man how he is still here">><<nobr>><<set $vitriolTalked to true>>
//Didn't drink,// he croaks. //That's the whole trick of staying, down here — not drinking.// He works
his cracked mouth. //You'll be for the castle. Everyone's for the castle. And you'll have seen the gate
— that curtain of sulphur-fire — and reckoned there's nothing for it but to burn through and pay in your
own skin.// He shakes his head, slow. //There's another road. The vents lay //cold// ones — where two of
their breaths lean together, the crust runs cold and bears weight, a bridge the fire never crosses. Go
read the crust at the nine throats //before// you pay the gate. I didn't, the once it mattered. Look at
the once it mattered.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The bone-rimmer has gone back to not-drinking, white hands folded at the green water's edge; he told you
the road, and will not be drawn out of his thirst again.
<</if>>

<<link "Toward a cinderfall">><<goto "TheCinderfall">><</link>>
<<link "Back across the fuming field">><<goto "TheSolfatara">><</link>>


:: TheGypsumWalk [hell]
A walk of gypsum runs from the cold shelf out toward the castle — the softer mineral the
vents also throw, pale and satiny and almost soothing underfoot after the brittle treachery
of the sulphur crust, laid in long drifts like dirty snow.

It gives underfoot the way snow gives, a soft crunch and a little yielding, and after a hundred
yards of it your shoulders come down off your ears for the first time since you reached the
bottom. The drifts are heaped and rounded and faintly luminous, the satiny grey-white of old
plaster, and the breath of the vents barely reaches here, so that the air is merely stale rather
than wounding, and the roar is far enough off to be almost a hush. You could believe, for a few
steps, that you were somewhere that meant you no harm.

It is the one stretch of the Deep that does not actively threaten you, and so of course you do
not trust it, and walk it with your eyes down and your nerves up, waiting for the trick, which
is that there is no trick, which is its own kind of unnerving in a place where everything else
is one.

<<if not $gypsumKnown>>
A mild, slow thing kneels in the gypsum drifts, eating the soft pale mineral by the handful, content as
nothing else in the Deep is content — the one soul here that found the harmless stretch and simply
stopped.
<<linkreplace "Ask the eater why he stopped">><<nobr>><<set $gypsumKnown to true>>
//Why'd I stop?// He considers it, mouth working the chalk. //Why'd I keep //going?// There's the gate,
the castle, the lord, the long climb — and there's //this:// a whole drift of it, soft and quiet, asking
nothing.// He scoops another handful. //That's the thing nobody tells you about the bottom — it doesn't
need a //gate// to keep you. Most don't. It just feeds your wanting till the wanting's all that's left,
and then a man'll stop anywhere there's enough to want at hand. I'm the lord's larder, same as everyone;
I'm just the part that's stopped minding being kept. Warm crust. Soft snow. The next handful. You'll see
how reasonable it gets.//
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "pig" 4>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The eater has shuffled a yard down the drift, mouth still working the pale mineral, and does not notice
you came back; the next handful is the whole of his horizon now, and it is enough.
<</if>>

<<link "Across a stretch of canary crust">><<goto "TheCanaryCrust">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the cold shelf">><<goto "TheCrust">><</link>>


:: TheCanaryCrust [hell]
A wide stretch of the brightest crust, pure sublimate, a canary-yellow so saturated it seems
to glow of its own accord, beautiful and uniform and stretching off into the haze toward the
castle's bulk. It is the picture-postcard of the Deep, the view the whole bottom of hell would
put on a flag if hell flew flags.

But you have learned, by now, what the brightness means. Out here there are no ridges, no
seams, no cold pale shelves to read — just the one perfect even sheet of yellow, unbroken to
the haze, which is exactly the look of crust that has frozen thinnest over the deepest heat,
nothing churning it, nothing thickening it, only the breath below holding it taut as the skin
on cooling milk. The warmth comes up through it soft and constant, the same warmth, you know,
that the thin places have, the same warmth the lying-down places have.

It is the thinnest and most treacherous ground there is — the bright even crust over the worst
of the heat — and a part of you that has been down here too long looks at all that warm bright
yellow and thinks how nice it would be to just lie down.

<<link "Out across the thin bright crust">><<goto "TheThinCrust">><</link>>
<<link "Back along the gypsum walk">><<goto "TheGypsumWalk">><</link>>
<<link "Across the fuming field">><<goto "TheSolfatara">><</link>>


:: TheCinderfall [hell]
<<roomart "digger">>
The far edge of the Deep, where the yellow gives out against a wall of old cinder — the slag
and clinker of the vents' oldest workings, heaped and fused into a black cliff that is, after
all the canary brightness, almost restful to the eye. There is no way on here. The Deep ends
against this black wall the way the world ends against the Deep: abruptly, without ceremony,
a thing simply stopping because there is nothing past it. You have reached, you realize,
standing at the cinder-wall, the actual bottom. There is no further down. The only direction
left, from the lowest place there is, is the long way back up.

And there is someone here, at the bottom, working — because of course there is; there is no place
so low that the deep has not found labour for it. Against the black cinder-wall a figure is
shovelling the old slag, lifting it and turning it and setting it down a yard from where it was,
with a flat-bladed shovel and the unhurried completeness of a body that has done a thing so long
that the doing is the whole of what's left of it. He is green, and soft, and broad in the face,
with the wet patient eyes of something amphibian — a man grown //down// into a frog the way a rat
grows up into a rat-man — and he wears a dented tin hat against nothing, and a labourer's apron
gone stiff and grey, and he does not stop when you come crunching across the cinders, because feet
coming and going stopped being news to him a very long time ago.

<<if not $diggerTalked>>
He hasn't looked up. You could leave him to it. Or you could do the thing nobody down here does
for anybody, and stop, and speak to him.
<<linkreplace "Stop, and speak to the old digger">>
He straightens — it costs him something — and looks at you, and there is no fear in the look and no
hope in it either, only a flat tired recognition: one labourer clocking another.

//You signed too, then,// he says. Not a question. //Everybody signs. You swear blind you won't,
right up to the part where you do.// He works his wide mouth at the cinder-air. //I dug trenches up
top, in the war. Then I died in one — went over and came down, and there wasn't even a join in it,
just trench and then trench. Been digging ever since. Somewhere along the way I stopped being able
to tell which trench is which, or that there was ever meant to be a difference.//

He looks at you a beat too long. //Have we…? No. No.// He shakes the dented hat. //Everyone looks
the same down here, after a while.//

And then he does the one kindness he has left to do: he leans on the shovel and tips the hat out
across the whole black bottom of the Deep, toward the single moving light a long way off — the one
warm thing in all that yellow and dark.

//Watch out for that tree over there,// he says. //The fire on it never goes out, and it'll burn
through anything. And I mean //anything// — clog, gate, wall, the bottom of a kettle, the back of
your own hand if you're fool enough to test it.// He sets the shovel to the slag again. //If you've
still got somewhere you're set on getting //to,// and something's gone and got in the way of it —
well. Now you know where there's a fire that won't be stopped.//
<<set $diggerTalked to true>>
<</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The old digger has gone back into the rhythm of the shovel and will not be drawn out of it again;
he said his piece, and tipped his hat at the far burning tree, and that was the whole of what he
had to give. The slag-wall takes the sound of the shovel and gives nothing back.
<</if>>

<<link "Back toward the standing vitriol">><<goto "TheVitriol">><</link>>


/* ---------- CLEAN-VS-DIRTY FORK: the fume-cut (gas-mask) — owner 2026-06-07 ----------
   Two ways to the tribute-niche: the GUARDED high cold-crust ledge (a pig-demon holds it — fight OR
   pay) or the DISCREET low fume-cut straight across the vent-breath (clean with the oilskin hood,
   else the bad air burns its way into you). New optional pocket; loops back to the vent-field. */

:: TheFumeCut [hell]
<<nobr>>
Between two of the roaring vents a cut runs low across the crust, a trough scoured where the two
breaths cross and scour, and the air down in it shimmers and warps like the air over a hot road.
Past it, on the far side, something glitters.

It resolves as you study it: a tribute-niche, a pocket in the crust where sulphur has grown a
yellow rind over offerings the pig-things left their lord — coin and worse, never collected,
glinting through the rind in the up-glow, cold and untouched a body's-length and one bad breath
away. There are two ways to it, and you can see them both from here. The high way around is a
cold-crust ledge, safe from the fumes; but a pig-demon lounges on it, picking its tusks, and the
only way past it is past it. The low way is the cut itself — straight through where the two vents
breathe across each other, a wall of shimmering chemical air you could walk if you didn't mind
what one lungful of it does.
<</nobr>>

<<if $ventCacheLooted>>
You've taken the niche already; the pig-demon has wandered off, the fume-cut breathes on, empty.
<<link "Across to the tribute-niche">><<goto "TheVentCache">><</link>>
<<else>>
//The price of each way is plain from here: the pig-demon wants a real fight, or ''24 coins'' to let you by; and the fume-cut would burn ''about 18 pig'' of bad air into your head<<if setup.has("gasmask")>> — but the hood takes that for you<</if>>.//

<<scentof "pigdemon">>
<<link "The guarded way — put the pig-demon down and take the high ledge">>
  <<run setup.startFight("pigdemon", "TheVentCache")>><<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<if $coins gte 24>>
<<link "The guarded way — buy the pig-demon's leave (24 coins), cross high and clean">>
  <<set $coins to $coins - 24>><<goto "TheVentCache">>
<</link>>
<<else>>(The pig-demon will move for 24 coins; you are short.)<</if>>
<<if setup.has("gasmask")>>
<<link "The discreet way — walk the fume-cut (the hood takes the bad air)">>
  <<goto "TheVentCache">>
<</link>>
<<else>>
<<set _tlbl to "The discreet way — walk the fume-cut bare-faced (" + setup.tollTag("head", "pig", 18) + ")">><<link _tlbl>>
  <<corrupt "head" "pig" 18>>
  <<goto "TheVentCache">>
<</link>>
<</if>>
<</if>>
<<link "Back to the field of vents">><<goto "TheVentField">><</link>>


:: TheVentCache [hell]
<<nobr>>
The niche is a pocket of cold air in all that heat, a still little hollow the cross-draught of
the two vents has somehow walled off from itself, quiet after the shimmer and scour of the cut.

The offerings are still in it. Sulphur has grown a yellow rind over everything, fusing the heap
into one lumpen golden mass, but the shapes underneath are clear enough — the round of a coin,
the curve of something that was a cup, the small hard outlines of things carried down and set
here and never thought of again. Nobody living has come to claim them in a long time. The rind
is unbroken, and the cold air smells of nothing but old metal and the faint sweet poison of the
crust itself.
<</nobr>>

<<if not $ventCacheLooted>>
<<set $ventCacheLooted to true>>
<<set _g to setup.gainCoins(random(14, 22))>>
You crack the rind and take <<= _g>> coin<<if _g neq 1>>s<</if>> from the hoard.
<<else>>
The niche is bare now, just yellow rind and cold air.
<</if>>

<<link "Back to the field of vents">><<goto "TheVentField">><</link>>


/* ---------- LAYER-9 BAD ENDS ---------- */

:: EndingSublimed [ending]
<<run setup.recordRun("died")>>
You lie down on the warm bright crust, because you are so tired, and the warmth comes up
through the thin yellow skin of the world soft as a blanket from a fire, and for one whole
breath it is the most comfortable you have been since the night by the drain. Then the crust,
which was only ever a held breath over the heat, lets the breath go.

It does not hurt the way you brace for. The heat takes you faster than nerves can report it —
you do not so much burn as *cease*, sublimed like the sulphur itself, a body gone straight to
yellow vapour and carried up one of the nine roaring throats into the dark, scattered across
the bottom of hell as one more breath of brimstone, indistinguishable now from the place that
took you.

//You lay down on the warm bright skin of the world, and the world breathed you in. The Deep
is made of everything that lay down. Now it is made of a little more.//

<<link "Again">><<run setup.restartCarry()>><</link>>


:: EndingHeir [ending]
<<run setup.recordRun("sovereign")>>
You take the other offer. You are so close to the bottom of yourself by now that the lord's
patience feels less like a trap than like being finally, correctly *seen* — and when a thing
as old as appetite offers to make you more than harvest, the part of you the descent has been
quietly rendering all this way says yes before the rest of you can vote.

The lord does not move to crown you. There is no crown. There is only the slow turning-over
of the farm's deed, the understanding settling into you like sulphur into crust: that you will
go up — it *wants* you to go up, that is the whole point of an heir who knows the way — and you
will walk the surface where the harvest grows thickest, and you will send it down, the
solitary and the undefended, the way the boar sent you down, because the farm is old and
hungry and now, in part, it is yours.

//You stayed, and were made more than harvest. The lord of the pigs has an heir who knows the
road to the surface — and the surface does not yet know it has a new and patient farmer.//

<<link "Again">><<run setup.restartCarry()>><</link>>
